Compilers and CPUs can reorder instructions for performance, as long as the reordering doesn't change the outcome for a single thread. However, this reordering can become problematic when multiple threads access shared memory. For example, a compiler might reorder a write to a flag *before* a write to shared data, even if the programmer intended the data write to happen first. Another thread observing the flag might then read inconsistent data. Memory barriers (fences) are needed to enforce a specific ordering of memory operations across threads.
```c
// Original intent: write data, then set flag
shared_data = value;
is_ready = true;

// Compiler/CPU reordering might result in:
is_ready = true;
shared_data = value; // If another thread reads is_ready=true here,
                     // it might read stale/uninitialized shared_data.
```
